acy from him.”  This was called the amir-a’yan system, with the “the caliph theoretically at the top, then the various sultans or other autonomous rulers supposedly acting as his agents, then the commander (amir) of their military forces, then the notables (a’yan) from the indigenous populace who mediated between conquerors and conquered, and lastly the populace.”  The caliph was not at the head of administration but did theoretically head the religious and military hierarchies.<br>Sultan Malik-Shah (r. 1073-92) introduced a new definition of caliphal authority and a separation of powers. From then on "the (Abbasid) caliph functioned as the religious head of Sunnism, while the (Seljuk) sultan, as its secular authority, enforced public order." <br>1. Caliph<br>2. Sultan. The title \'sultan\', "carried with it the notion of defender of the faith". <br>3. Jurists. The central concern of the madrasa was the study of law in this period. <br>3. Imams<br>'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1073, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 898})
